(typeof module==="object"&&module.exports?require("../curriculum.js"):BodyguardCurriculum).register(2,[["q2-a13ec86d3e7d","s2.0-c6f57180e462","2.0","Introduction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsmallest unit of an element that retains the unique properties of that element","atom",["base","nucleotide","inorganic compound"],"atom: smallest unit of an element that retains the unique properties of that element.","The answer begins with A and has 1 word.","eip-id1165578232768","f-2ca2b7e51a27"],["q2-479af18b87e3","s2.0-1405df66cbe2","2.0","Introduction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncompound that accepts hydrogen ions (H⁺ ) in solution","base",["matter","nucleotide","atom"],"base: compound that accepts hydrogen ions (H⁺ ) in solution.","The answer begins with B and has 1 word.","fs-id2033651","f-92b0dea83d1c"],["q2-51c0138d3e85","s2.0-8c7bb9f61d23","2.0","Introduction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nphysical substance; that which occupies space and has mass","matter",["nucleotide","base","deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA)"],"matter: physical substance; that which occupies space and has mass.","The answer begins with M and has 1 word.","eip-id1165578232768","f-a4c732e4d9f5"],["q2-7b12f83f2a26","s2.0-31f1e25a1b1d","2.0","Introduction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nclass of organic compounds composed of one or more phosphate groups, a pentose sugar, and a base","nucleotide",["matter","atom","isotope"],"nucleotide: class of organic compounds composed of one or more phosphate groups, a pentose sugar, and a base.","The answer begins with N and has 1 word.","fs-id2033651","f-b10ae882ec14"],["q2-95953ee33bb4","s2.0-31f1e25a1b1d","2.0","Introduction","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn fact, chemicals called ____ bases are the foundation of the genetic code with the instructions on how to build and maintain the human body from conception through old age.","nucleotide",["matter","base","atom"],"In fact, chemicals called nucleotide bases are the foundation of the genetic code with the instructions on how to build and maintain the human body from conception through old age.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2033651","f-6e9738a8c12b"],["q2-736297244cbe","s2.1-040f765e48ff","2.1","Atomic Number and Mass Number","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nnumber of protons in the nucleus of an atom","atomic number",["radioactive isotope","valence shell","electron shell"],"atomic number: number of protons in the nucleus of an atom.","The answer begins with A and has 2 words.","fs-id1962969","f-8c15aa6923db"],["q2-4152144ee7ad","s2.1-c2e2f8015078","2.1","Elements and Compounds","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsubstance composed of two or more different elements joined by chemical bonds","compound",["proton","neutron","pH"],"compound: substance composed of two or more different elements joined by chemical bonds.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id1882474","f-3dc47f70ce03"],["q2-e8f3baab8d1a","s2.1-da005969e9d1","2.1","Atomic Structure and Energy","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsubatomic particle having a negative charge and nearly no mass; found orbiting the atom’s nucleus","electron",["element","matter","isotope"],"electron: subatomic particle having a negative charge and nearly no mass; found orbiting the atom’s nucleus.","The answer begins with E and has 1 word.","fs-id1484653","f-0a1042b2388e"],["q2-3d6097b10772","s2.1-d86943bbc756","2.1","The Behavior of Electrons","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\narea of space a given distance from an atom’s nucleus in which electrons are grouped","electron shell",["atomic number","mass number","valence shell"],"electron shell: area of space a given distance from an atom’s nucleus in which electrons are grouped.","The answer begins with E and has 2 words.","fs-id1391723","f-900d49af565b"],["q2-dd59c56380a8","s2.1-dece05484839","2.1","Elements and Compounds","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsubstance that cannot be created or broken down by ordinary chemical means","element",["proton","isotope","kinetic energy"],"element: substance that cannot be created or broken down by ordinary chemical means.","The answer begins with E and has 1 word.","fs-id2052014","f-166593ec8665"],["q2-ab197ea84c31","s2.1-5e451c08c838","2.1","Isotopes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\none of the variations of an element in which the number of neutrons differ from each other","isotope",["proton","molecule","matter"],"isotope: one of the variations of an element in which the number of neutrons differ from each other.","The answer begins with I and has 1 word.","fs-id2036643","f-d9667180b1c4"],["q2-fecbefa971c9","s2.1-4d1d42ede190","2.1","Atomic Number and Mass Number","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsum of the number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus of an atom","mass number",["atomic number","valence shell","electron shell"],"mass number: sum of the number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus of an atom.","The answer begins with M and has 2 words.","fs-id2673650","f-cf6a201c81b2"],["q2-83315afb497a","s2.1-b5d0c80a5b51","2.1","The Behavior of Electrons","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ntwo or more atoms covalently bonded together","molecule",["neutron","deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA)","potential energy"],"molecule: two or more atoms covalently bonded together.","The answer begins with M and has 1 word.","fs-id1616535","f-ebbb036d163a"],["q2-7074544923d8","s2.1-f96b413b1cae","2.1","Atomic Structure and Energy","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nheavy subatomic particle having no electrical charge and found in the atom’s nucleus","neutron",["electron","molecule","proton"],"neutron: heavy subatomic particle having no electrical charge and found in the atom’s nucleus.","The answer begins with N and has 1 word.","fs-id1484653","f-c21828b853d2"],["q2-c045084cea1c","s2.1-23ca611f82b4","2.1","Atomic Number and Mass Number","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\narrangement of the elements in a table according to their atomic number; elements having similar properties because of their electron arrangements compose columns in the table, while elements having the same number of valence shells compose rows in the table","periodic table of the elements",["deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA)","ribonucleic acid (RNA)","adenosine triphosphate (ATP)"],"periodic table of the elements: arrangement of the elements in a table according to their atomic number; elements having similar properties because of their electron arrangements compose columns in the table, while elements having the same number of valence shells compose rows in the table.","The answer begins with P and has 5 words.","fs-id2364434","f-82f321ee4847"],["q2-3baa653ac25a","s2.1-502648de665b","2.1","Atomic Structure and Energy","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nheavy subatomic particle having a positive charge and found in the atom’s nucleus","proton",["compound","molecule","electron"],"proton: heavy subatomic particle having a positive charge and found in the atom’s nucleus.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id1484653","f-2cd047dcacf3"],["q2-55b27c4def2b","s2.1-3f89e51ccfa5","2.1","Isotopes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nunstable, heavy isotope that gives off subatomic particles, or electromagnetic energy, as it decays; also called radioisotopes","radioactive isotope",["electron shell","mass number","atomic number"],"radioactive isotope: unstable, heavy isotope that gives off subatomic particles, or electromagnetic energy, as it decays; also called radioisotopes.","The answer begins with R and has 2 words.","fs-id1418017","f-263691eb230b"],["q2-c335f0718e64","s2.1-518ae2637462","2.1","The Behavior of Electrons","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\noutermost electron shell of an atom","valence shell",["radioactive isotope","mass number","atomic number"],"valence shell: outermost electron shell of an atom.","The answer begins with V and has 2 words.","fs-id1850994","f-bd6aad35db14"],["q2-0954b91c91a1","s2.1-8c7bb9f61d23","2.1","Elements and Atoms: The Building Blocks of Matter","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe substance of the universe—from a grain of sand to a star—is called ____.","matter",["isotope","electron","pyrimidine"],"The substance of the universe—from a grain of sand to a star—is called matter.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2242606","f-6dccb226eda1"],["q2-f34cc49420b9","s2.1-8c7bb9f61d23","2.1","Elements and Atoms: The Building Blocks of Matter","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nScientists define ____ as anything that occupies space and has mass.","matter",["proton","isotope","monosaccharide"],"Scientists define matter as anything that occupies space and has mass.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2242606","f-30ea46ec606f"],["q2-aae45f91a6fb","s2.1-8c7bb9f61d23","2.1","Elements and Atoms: The Building Blocks of Matter","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn object’s mass is the amount of ____ contained in the object, and the object’s mass is the same whether that object is on Earth or in the zero-gravity environment of outer space.","matter",["isotope","potential energy","periodic table of the elements"],"An object’s mass is the amount of matter contained in the object, and the object’s mass is the same whether that object is on Earth or in the zero-gravity environment of outer space.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2242606","f-18d49cee4292"],["q2-4fb8d9f8aa45","s2.1-8c7bb9f61d23","2.1","Elements and Compounds","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAll ____ in the natural world is composed of one or more of the 92 fundamental substances called elements.","matter",["neutron","electron","potential energy"],"All matter in the natural world is composed of one or more of the 92 fundamental substances called elements.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2052014","f-2eec8568e9f9"],["q2-fca5e3ec5221","s2.1-dece05484839","2.1","Elements and Compounds","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA familiar example of an ____ that you must take in is calcium (Ca).","element",["ion","phosphorylation","exchange reaction"],"A familiar example of an element that you must take in is calcium (Ca).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2052014","f-1ddd5b42acee"],["q2-673cbf931d9a","s2.1-c2e2f8015078","2.1","Elements and Compounds","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is a substance composed of two or more elements joined by chemical bonds.","compound",["proton","neutron","synthesis reaction"],"A compound is a substance composed of two or more elements joined by chemical bonds.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1882474","f-4a03d52fef32"],["q2-3467827a0698","s2.1-c2e2f8015078","2.1","Elements and Compounds","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ glucose is an important body fuel.","compound",["isotope","triglyceride","potential energy"],"For example, the compound glucose is an important body fuel.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1882474","f-d436c4a9d1fa"],["q2-f2ebf3a4d52b","s2.1-c2e2f8015078","2.1","Elements and Compounds","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nMoreover, the elements that make up any given ____ always occur in the same relative amounts.","compound",["isotope","ionic bond","nucleotide"],"Moreover, the elements that make up any given compound always occur in the same relative amounts.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1882474","f-bd00672f0a53"],["q2-01c65e7f0dd0","s2.1-c6f57180e462","2.1","Atoms and Subatomic Particles","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn other words, an ____ of hydrogen is a unit of hydrogen—the smallest amount of hydrogen that can exist.","atom",["neutron","phosphorylation","phospholipid"],"In other words, an atom of hydrogen is a unit of hydrogen—the smallest amount of hydrogen that can exist.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2094276","f-42dd4a6fd856"],["q2-db1b026d004a","s2.1-da005969e9d1","2.1","Atomic Structure and Energy","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAtoms are made up of even smaller subatomic particles, three types of which are important: the proton, neutron, and ____.","electron",["element","isotope","molecule"],"Atoms are made up of even smaller subatomic particles, three types of which are important: the proton, neutron, and electron.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1484653","f-7369bd3c6671"],["q2-b84755848ea0","s2.1-dece05484839","2.1","Atomic Structure and Energy","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe number of positively-charged protons and non-charged (“neutral”) neutrons, gives mass to the atom, and the number of protons determines the ____.","element",["proton","monosaccharide","phospholipid"],"The number of positively-charged protons and non-charged (“neutral”) neutrons, gives mass to the atom, and the number of protons determines the element.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1484653","f-478bb8e40b6a"],["q2-502f4131ab90","s2.1-da005969e9d1","2.1","Atomic Structure and Energy","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn ____ has about 1/2000th the mass of a proton or neutron.","electron",["atom","matter","molecule"],"An electron has about 1/2000th the mass of a proton or neutron.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1484653","f-0ca098d4e292"],["q2-2f8e5e6c04bd","s2.1-c6f57180e462","2.1","Atomic Structure and Energy","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn ____’s protons and electrons carry electrical charges.","atom",["neutron","anion","exchange reaction"],"An atom’s protons and electrons carry electrical charges.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1836659","f-fe806fc17d52"],["q2-beb55bda6d2e","s2.1-502648de665b","2.1","Atomic Number and Mass Number","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn atom of carbon is unique to carbon, but a ____ of carbon is not.","proton",["isotope","compound","element"],"An atom of carbon is unique to carbon, but a proton of carbon is not.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1962969","f-c9ec8980d02d"],["q2-96af6582db26","s2.1-dece05484839","2.1","Atomic Number and Mass Number","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nCarbon by definition is an ____ whose atoms contain six protons.","element",["electron","concentration","pyrimidine"],"Carbon by definition is an element whose atoms contain six protons.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1962969","f-b6e10f3a3c5e"],["q2-6a04497b8328","s2.1-040f765e48ff","2.1","Atomic Number and Mass Number","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThus, the ____, which is the number of protons in the nucleus of the atom, identifies the element.","atomic number",["mass number","radioactive isotope","valence shell"],"Thus, the atomic number, which is the number of protons in the nucleus of the atom, identifies the element.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1962969","f-15b9a6879117"],["q2-181fe5dac61a","s2.1-040f765e48ff","2.1","Atomic Number and Mass Number","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nBecause an atom usually has the same number of electrons as protons, the ____ identifies the usual number of electrons as well.","atomic number",["radioactive isotope","mass number","valence shell"],"Because an atom usually has the same number of electrons as protons, the atomic number identifies the usual number of electrons as well.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1962969","f-7ff5e32b34a3"],["q2-4c01cc9d0b3f","s2.1-dece05484839","2.1","Atomic Number and Mass Number","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe number of protons and electrons in an ____ are equal.","element",["neutron","isotope","electron"],"The number of protons and electrons in an element are equal.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2364434","f-5b2f82911852"],["q2-b01fc0fb3a42","s2.1-23ca611f82b4","2.1","Elements and Atoms: The Building Blocks of Matter","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn the ____, elements in a single column have the same number of electrons that can participate in a chemical reaction.","periodic table of the elements",["ribonucleic acid (RNA)","deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA)","adenosine triphosphate (ATP)"],"In the periodic table of the elements, elements in a single column have the same number of electrons that can participate in a chemical reaction.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2500093","f-0c365362d81d"],["q2-a6eb69c2804e","s2.1-4d1d42ede190","2.1","Isotopes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe standard isotope of carbon is 12 C, commonly called carbon twelve. 12 C has six protons and six neutrons, for a ____ of twelve.","mass number",["radioactive isotope","atomic number","electron shell"],"The standard isotope of carbon is 12 C, commonly called carbon twelve. 12 C has six protons and six neutrons, for a mass number of twelve.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2036643","f-d0ffd297e161"],["q2-d6782425ca7d","s2.1-3f89e51ccfa5","2.1","Isotopes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is an isotope whose nucleus readily decays, giving off subatomic particles and electromagnetic energy.","radioactive isotope",["mass number","atomic number","electron shell"],"A radioactive isotope is an isotope whose nucleus readily decays, giving off subatomic particles and electromagnetic energy.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1418017","f-acab37f53ec0"],["q2-1811fdd439ee","s2.1-d86943bbc756","2.1","The Behavior of Electrons","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn ____ is a layer of electrons that encircle the nucleus at a distinct energy level.","electron shell",["radioactive isotope","mass number","valence shell"],"An electron shell is a layer of electrons that encircle the nucleus at a distinct energy level.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1391723","f-617004e7cb6c"],["q2-368ed75d816f","s2.1-c6f57180e462","2.1","The Behavior of Electrons","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe precise number of shells depends on the number of electrons in the ____.","atom",["proton","neutron","hydrogen bond"],"The precise number of shells depends on the number of electrons in the atom.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2603370","f-047dd05e9876"],["q2-60f59db536df","s2.1-040f765e48ff","2.1","The Behavior of Electrons","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nLithium (Li), whose ____ is 3, has three electrons.","atomic number",["mass number","valence shell","electron shell"],"Lithium (Li), whose atomic number is 3, has three electrons.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2070158","f-56d18bf494d2"],["q2-307d6a4d3d26","s2.1-da005969e9d1","2.1","The Behavior of Electrons","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAgain, a look at the periodic table reveals that all of the elements in the second row, from lithium to neon, have just two ____ shells.","electron",["isotope","molecule","matter"],"Again, a look at the periodic table reveals that all of the elements in the second row, from lithium to neon, have just two electron shells.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2070158","f-dfa06adbecef"],["q2-639530937a16","s2.1-d86943bbc756","2.1","The Behavior of Electrons","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA valence shell is an atom’s outermost ____.","electron shell",["radioactive isotope","mass number","atomic number"],"A valence shell is an atom’s outermost electron shell.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1850994","f-b94be9ab919a"],["q2-8c7b30e5c96c","s2.1-dece05484839","2.1","The Behavior of Electrons","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn nature, atoms of one ____ tend to join with atoms of other elements in characteristic ways.","element",["isotope","lipid","valence shell"],"In nature, atoms of one element tend to join with atoms of other elements in characteristic ways.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1890709","f-be9251f34020"],["q2-58a03a65b4fd","s2.2-ec762cb4a39b","2.2","Chemical Bonds","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\natom with a negative charge","anion",["cation","bond","element"],"anion: atom with a negative charge.","The answer begins with A and has 1 word.","fs-id2158913","f-9066a0b31662"],["q2-e6c7e4593007","s2.2-1d2bba5d0b80","2.2","Chemical Bonds","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nelectrical force linking atoms","bond",["anion","proton","compound"],"bond: electrical force linking atoms.","The answer begins with B and has 1 word.","fs-id1493624","f-86efad83827b"],["q2-f35e02c3a215","s2.2-9b5a1efc96e2","2.2","Chemical Bonds","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\natom with a positive charge","cation",["bond","proton","anion"],"cation: atom with a positive charge.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id2579813","f-e509a26b2835"],["q2-31fc772f99a8","s2.2-1bdf81c422c2","2.2","Covalent Bonds","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nchemical bond in which two atoms share electrons, thereby completing their valence shells","covalent bond",["atomic number","hydrogen bond","ionic bond"],"covalent bond: chemical bond in which two atoms share electrons, thereby completing their valence shells.","The answer begins with C and has 2 words.","fs-id2095610","f-dd05773d99b4"],["q2-eec551c8698c","s2.2-926a14b69be4","2.2","Polar Covalent Bonds","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndipole-dipole bond in which a hydrogen atom covalently bonded to an electronegative atom is weakly attracted to a second electronegative atom","hydrogen bond",["covalent bond","ionic bond","polar molecule"],"hydrogen bond: dipole-dipole bond in which a hydrogen atom covalently bonded to an electronegative atom is weakly attracted to a second electronegative atom.","The answer begins with H and has 2 words.","fs-id1278025","f-9cfbca1f1e79"],["q2-e9c5d927038b","s2.2-bbb286479461","2.2","Ions and Ionic Bonds","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\natom with an overall positive or negative charge","ion",["molecule","electron","compound"],"ion: atom with an overall positive or negative charge.","The answer begins with I and has 1 word.","fs-id2113058","f-8292593cea96"],["q2-d187ca6bc9d9","s2.2-a4ef1c94a4b0","2.2","Chemical Bonds","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nattraction between an anion and a cation","ionic bond",["electron shell","hydrogen bond","covalent bond"],"ionic bond: attraction between an anion and a cation.","The answer begins with I and has 2 words.","fs-id1898670","f-4c00033ba532"],["q2-bcbd46d8b6a7","s2.2-3a0372c1ccec","2.2","Polar Covalent Bonds","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmolecule with regions that have opposite charges resulting from uneven numbers of electrons in the nuclei of the atoms participating in the covalent bond","polar molecule",["valence shell","electron shell","ionic bond"],"polar molecule: molecule with regions that have opposite charges resulting from uneven numbers of electrons in the nuclei of the atoms participating in the covalent bond.","The answer begins with P and has 2 words.","fs-id1909147","f-efe40c3a032e"],["q2-202ce22f9a63","s2.2-1d2bba5d0b80","2.2","Chemical Bonds","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is a weak or strong electrical attraction that holds atoms in the same vicinity.","bond",["element","compound","anion"],"A bond is a weak or strong electrical attraction that holds atoms in the same vicinity.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1493624","f-a793752e2678"],["q2-4befa695e7e1","s2.2-b5d0c80a5b51","2.2","Chemical Bonds","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA more or less stable grouping of two or more atoms held together by chemical bonds is called a ____.","molecule",["anion","bond","amino acid"],"A more or less stable grouping of two or more atoms held together by chemical bonds is called a molecule.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1493624","f-bdcff6d8f971"],["q2-4422ebf94a96","s2.2-dece05484839","2.2","Chemical Bonds","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe bonded atoms may be of the same ____, as in the case of H 2, which is called molecular hydrogen or hydrogen gas.","element",["ion","anion","prostaglandin"],"The bonded atoms may be of the same element, as in the case of H 2, which is called molecular hydrogen or hydrogen gas.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1493624","f-1fdddda8b1f3"],["q2-fb7632412dd3","s2.2-b5d0c80a5b51","2.2","Chemical Bonds","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThus, a unit of water, or H 2 O, is a compound, as is a single ____ of the gas methane, or CH 4.","molecule",["anion","ion","cation"],"Thus, a unit of water, or H 2 O, is a compound, as is a single molecule of the gas methane, or CH 4.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1493624","f-8156ef7a1d05"],["q2-701267719641","s2.2-c6f57180e462","2.2","Ions and Ionic Bonds","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nRecall that an ____ typically has the same number of positively charged protons and negatively charged electrons.","atom",["cation","anion","synthesis reaction"],"Recall that an atom typically has the same number of positively charged protons and negatively charged electrons.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2113058","f-7c82291452a4"],["q2-c0e04a7148fc","s2.2-c6f57180e462","2.2","Ions and Ionic Bonds","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn ____ that has an electrical charge—whether positive or negative—is an ion.","atom",["anion","electron","kinetic energy"],"An atom that has an electrical charge—whether positive or negative—is an ion.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2113058","f-f9bc22fe99bd"],["q2-947c38a51c8c","s2.2-dece05484839","2.2","Chemical Bonds","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nPotassium (K), for instance, is an important ____ in all body cells.","element",["electron","anion","acid"],"Potassium (K), for instance, is an important element in all body cells.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2579813","f-63c696ab071a"],["q2-012d2df73ab2","s2.2-9b5a1efc96e2","2.2","Chemical Bonds","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA positively charged ion is known as a ____.","cation",["electron","compound","bond"],"A positively charged ion is known as a cation.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2579813","f-13ceb776d1fb"],["q2-004ca553d863","s2.2-ec762cb4a39b","2.2","Chemical Bonds","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA negatively charged ion is known as an ____.","anion",["proton","cation","molecule"],"A negatively charged ion is known as an anion.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2158913","f-ec2958dfffb3"],["q2-0b1954736f9e","s2.2-da005969e9d1","2.2","Chemical Bonds","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAtoms that have more than one ____ to donate or accept will end up with stronger positive or negative charges.","electron",["compound","bond","atom"],"Atoms that have more than one electron to donate or accept will end up with stronger positive or negative charges.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1405066","f-5bddf1cf05b8"],["q2-56ef952c78b9","s2.2-9b5a1efc96e2","2.2","Chemical Bonds","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ that has donated two electrons has a net charge of +2.","cation",["molecule","element","bond"],"A cation that has donated two electrons has a net charge of +2.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1405066","f-72dbd3f0ae45"],["q2-5d4f5d89344b","s2.2-ec762cb4a39b","2.2","Chemical Bonds","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn ____ that has accepted two electrons has a net charge of –2.","anion",["bond","element","molecule"],"An anion that has accepted two electrons has a net charge of –2.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1405066","f-d0c7b17756fd"],["q2-779969f917ff","s2.2-a4ef1c94a4b0","2.2","Chemical Bonds","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe opposite charges of cations and anions exert a moderately strong mutual attraction that keeps the atoms in close proximity forming an ____.","ionic bond",["hydrogen bond","covalent bond","electron shell"],"The opposite charges of cations and anions exert a moderately strong mutual attraction that keeps the atoms in close proximity forming an ionic bond.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1898670","f-e3199e29cf31"],["q2-60728e067ad7","s2.2-a4ef1c94a4b0","2.2","Chemical Bonds","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn ____ is an ongoing, close association between ions of opposite charge.","ionic bond",["atomic number","electron shell","covalent bond"],"An ionic bond is an ongoing, close association between ions of opposite charge.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1898670","f-c2aa93b0612a"],["q2-3f377b5d2ede","s2.2-1bdf81c422c2","2.2","Covalent Bonds","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nUnlike ionic bonds formed by the attraction between a cation’s positive charge and an anion’s negative charge, molecules formed by a ____ share electrons in a mutually stabilizing relationship.","covalent bond",["polar molecule","ionic bond","hydrogen bond"],"Unlike ionic bonds formed by the attraction between a cation’s positive charge and an anion’s negative charge, molecules formed by a covalent bond share electrons in a mutually stabilizing relationship.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2095610","f-5599683b3d62"],["q2-bd3188a07b57","s2.2-da005969e9d1","2.2","Nonpolar Covalent Bonds","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nNotice that the two covalently bonded atoms typically share just one or two ____ pairs, though larger sharings are possible.","electron",["ion","bond","molecule"],"Notice that the two covalently bonded atoms typically share just one or two electron pairs, though larger sharings are possible.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2352651","f-5780195f1906"],["q2-e856d4c1e936","s2.2-da005969e9d1","2.2","Nonpolar Covalent Bonds","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThere even are triple covalent bonds, where three ____ pairs are shared between two atoms.","electron",["proton","element","compound"],"There even are triple covalent bonds, where three electron pairs are shared between two atoms.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2352651","f-f8e29f6494ef"],["q2-da230cea374b","s2.2-3a0372c1ccec","2.2","Polar Covalent Bonds","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn chemistry, a ____ is a molecule that contains regions that have opposite electrical charges.","polar molecule",["atomic number","hydrogen bond","covalent bond"],"In chemistry, a polar molecule is a molecule that contains regions that have opposite electrical charges.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1909147","f-36ba0e6a9adc"],["q2-92292401564b","s2.2-b5d0c80a5b51","2.2","Polar Covalent Bonds","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ has three parts: one atom of oxygen, the nucleus of which contains eight protons, and two hydrogen atoms, whose nuclei each contain only one proton.","molecule",["ion","electron","cation"],"The molecule has three parts: one atom of oxygen, the nucleus of which contains eight protons, and two hydrogen atoms, whose nuclei each contain only one proton.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1490078","f-83ccceda17e9"],["q2-1fb77d4291c7","s2.2-b5d0c80a5b51","2.2","Hydrogen Bonds","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nHydrogen bonding occurs because the weakly negative oxygen atom in one water ____ is attracted to the weakly positive hydrogen atoms of two other water molecules.","molecule",["anion","solution","phospholipid"],"Hydrogen bonding occurs because the weakly negative oxygen atom in one water molecule is attracted to the weakly positive hydrogen atoms of two other water molecules.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1391982","f-c9c268a6bf01"],["q2-cbad3d0b6412","s2.3-434b0a6daa53","2.3","Enzymes and Other Catalysts","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncompound that releases hydrogen ions (H⁺ ) in solution","acid",["bond","product","catalyst"],"acid: compound that releases hydrogen ions (H⁺ ) in solution.","The answer begins with A and has 1 word.","fs-id1855242","f-de146fdedeb2"],["q2-4cd60e3c852c","s2.3-8b2e04d7ef91","2.3","Enzymes and Other Catalysts","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\namount of energy greater than the energy contained in the reactants, which must be overcome for a reaction to proceed","activation energy",["chemical energy","kinetic energy","exchange reaction"],"activation energy: amount of energy greater than the energy contained in the reactants, which must be overcome for a reaction to proceed.","The answer begins with A and has 2 words.","fs-id1855242","f-b7a0ddd6b8ec"],["q2-e0ef5111a0dc","s2.3-c6482363313d","2.3","Enzymes and Other Catalysts","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsubstance that increases the rate of a chemical reaction without itself being changed in the process","catalyst",["reactant","product","matter"],"catalyst: substance that increases the rate of a chemical reaction without itself being changed in the process.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id2371267","f-2f50dbc06599"],["q2-0d3cb26a6eb2","s2.3-e8467bcb65eb","2.3","The Role of Energy in Chemical Reactions","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nform of energy that is absorbed as chemical bonds form, stored as they are maintained, and released as they are broken","chemical energy",["potential energy","exchange reaction","synthesis reaction"],"chemical energy: form of energy that is absorbed as chemical bonds form, stored as they are maintained, and released as they are broken.","The answer begins with C and has 2 words.","fs-id1384002","f-b6b982d46e20"],["q2-962e6bcd124c","s2.3-66d80f5fad63","2.3","Concentration and Pressure","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nnumber of particles within a given space","concentration",["matter","atom","product"],"concentration: number of particles within a given space.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id2254825","f-6b8db291ee8e"],["q2-48e3cf348445","s2.3-1fb42c942bd1","2.3","Characteristics of Chemical Reactions","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ntype of catabolic reaction in which one or more bonds within a larger molecule are broken, resulting in the release of smaller molecules or atoms","decomposition reaction",["chemical energy","potential energy","synthesis reaction"],"decomposition reaction: type of catabolic reaction in which one or more bonds within a larger molecule are broken, resulting in the release of smaller molecules or atoms.","The answer begins with D and has 2 words.","fs-id2152461","f-7f6161c0f4da"],["q2-e8a4693182d3","s2.3-1e24e38c6148","2.3","Enzymes and Other Catalysts","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nprotein or RNA that catalyzes chemical reactions","enzyme",["concentration","compound","product"],"enzyme: protein or RNA that catalyzes chemical reactions.","The answer begins with E and has 1 word.","fs-id1855242","f-430fd5b16bd1"],["q2-98a3d3703918","s2.3-95855e4784d5","2.3","Characteristics of Chemical Reactions","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ntype of chemical reaction in which bonds are both formed and broken, resulting in the transfer of components","exchange reaction",["activation energy","valence shell","potential energy"],"exchange reaction: type of chemical reaction in which bonds are both formed and broken, resulting in the transfer of components.","The answer begins with E and has 2 words.","fs-id2110634","f-c21e0503bd7c"],["q2-6c54e942697f","s2.3-3ea71d906c7f","2.3","The Role of Energy in Chemical Reactions","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nenergy that matter possesses because of its motion","kinetic energy",["synthesis reaction","activation energy","decomposition reaction"],"kinetic energy: energy that matter possesses because of its motion.","The answer begins with K and has 2 words.","fs-id2033760","f-983277a697cc"],["q2-08ebc867c265","s2.3-f310feafda53","2.3","The Role of Energy in Chemical Reactions","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nstored energy matter possesses because of the positioning or structure of its components","potential energy",["exchange reaction","synthesis reaction","kinetic energy"],"potential energy: stored energy matter possesses because of the positioning or structure of its components.","The answer begins with P and has 2 words.","fs-id2033760","f-c8392cb85b67"],["q2-9952a6d602d7","s2.3-38a007151abe","2.3","Characteristics of Chemical Reactions","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\none or more substances produced by a chemical reaction","product",["catalyst","acid","bond"],"product: one or more substances produced by a chemical reaction.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id2081438","f-107773df9272"],["q2-cc1596ea7d8c","s2.3-2c65275ae0b0","2.3","Enzymes and Other Catalysts","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nclass of organic compounds that are composed of many amino acids linked together by peptide bonds","protein",["bond","atom","enzyme"],"protein: class of organic compounds that are composed of many amino acids linked together by peptide bonds.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id1855242","f-beeb2834cddd"],["q2-b2290151313f","s2.3-92aefdb02ffd","2.3","Characteristics of Chemical Reactions","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\none or more substances that enter into the reaction","reactant",["acid","catalyst","bond"],"reactant: one or more substances that enter into the reaction.","The answer begins with R and has 1 word.","fs-id2081438","f-892bc59706ff"],["q2-d35be10ab0a8","s2.3-cdbcbd2f1208","2.3","Enzymes and Other Catalysts","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nribose-containing nucleotide that helps manifest the genetic code as protein","ribonucleic acid (RNA)",["matter","enzyme","reactant"],"ribonucleic acid (RNA): ribose-containing nucleotide that helps manifest the genetic code as protein.","The answer begins with R and has 3 words.","fs-id1855242","f-5da458a1bc17"],["q2-ec7a477930a8","s2.3-0cc5d96a99e2","2.3","Characteristics of Chemical Reactions","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ntype of anabolic reaction in which two or more atoms or molecules bond, resulting in the formation of a larger molecule","synthesis reaction",["potential energy","decomposition reaction","kinetic energy"],"synthesis reaction: type of anabolic reaction in which two or more atoms or molecules bond, resulting in the formation of a larger molecule.","The answer begins with S and has 2 words.","fs-id2069935","f-a816f66b92a0"],["q2-2b1975daec11","s2.3-8c7bb9f61d23","2.3","Chemical Reactions","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nBoth types of reaction involve exchanges not only of ____, but of energy.","matter",["reactant","protein","acid"],"Both types of reaction involve exchanges not only of matter, but of energy.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1473680","f-c1ce01ff6885"],["q2-577bb9784c1b","s2.3-8c7bb9f61d23","2.3","The Role of Energy in Chemical Reactions","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nChemical reactions require a sufficient amount of energy to cause the ____ to collide with enough precision and force that old chemical bonds can be broken and new ones formed.","matter",["protein","product","adenosine triphosphate (ATP)"],"Chemical reactions require a sufficient amount of energy to cause the matter to collide with enough precision and force that old chemical bonds can be broken and new ones formed.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2033760","f-5a2d48f9f9d0"],["q2-1bbae5330b27","s2.3-3ea71d906c7f","2.3","The Role of Energy in Chemical Reactions","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn general, ____ is the form of energy powering any type of matter in motion.","kinetic energy",["valence shell","chemical energy","synthesis reaction"],"In general, kinetic energy is the form of energy powering any type of matter in motion.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2033760","f-d64e6e031c82"],["q2-c93aa7062baa","s2.3-f310feafda53","2.3","The Role of Energy in Chemical Reactions","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIf the brick wall collapses, the stored ____ is released as kinetic energy as the bricks fall.","potential energy",["activation energy","valence shell","chemical energy"],"If the brick wall collapses, the stored potential energy is released as kinetic energy as the bricks fall.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2033760","f-82444ab7ced5"],["q2-aae7080828de","s2.3-f310feafda53","2.3","The Role of Energy in Chemical Reactions","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn the human body, ____ is stored in the bonds between atoms and molecules.","potential energy",["exchange reaction","chemical energy","kinetic energy"],"In the human body, potential energy is stored in the bonds between atoms and molecules.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1384002","f-a4d6d74e45fb"],["q2-b895f44a4547","s2.3-f310feafda53","2.3","The Role of Energy in Chemical Reactions","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nChemical energy is the form of ____ in which energy is stored in chemical bonds.","potential energy",["decomposition reaction","activation energy","valence shell"],"Chemical energy is the form of potential energy in which energy is stored in chemical bonds.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1384002","f-773427e8dfe4"],["q2-d8d46b74962c","s2.3-3ea71d906c7f","2.3","The Role of Energy in Chemical Reactions","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nWhen you eat an energy bar before heading out the door for a hike, the honey, nuts, and other foods the bar contains are broken down and rearranged by your body into molecules that your muscle cells convert to ____.","kinetic energy",["exchange reaction","activation energy","valence shell"],"When you eat an energy bar before heading out the door for a hike, the honey, nuts, and other foods the bar contains are broken down and rearranged by your body into molecules that your muscle cells convert to kinetic energy.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1384002","f-6963f127fb4e"],["q2-fb48129aac03","s2.3-e8467bcb65eb","2.3","Forms of Energy Important in Human Functioning","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nYou have already learned that ____ is absorbed, stored, and released by chemical bonds.","chemical energy",["valence shell","synthesis reaction","decomposition reaction"],"You have already learned that chemical energy is absorbed, stored, and released by chemical bonds.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1880539","f-b70139caf09c"],["q2-aae715aab516","s2.3-e8467bcb65eb","2.3","Forms of Energy Important in Human Functioning","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nTo ____, mechanical, radiant, and electrical energy are important in human functioning.","chemical energy",["potential energy","kinetic energy","valence shell"],"In addition to chemical energy, mechanical, radiant, and electrical energy are important in human functioning.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1880539","f-cc175b2502a7"],["q2-3f56923e093f","s2.3-8c7bb9f61d23","2.3","Forms of Energy Important in Human Functioning","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nRadiant energy is energy emitted and transmitted as waves rather than ____.","matter",["acid","concentration","pH"],"Radiant energy is energy emitted and transmitted as waves rather than matter.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1637609","f-9651f2dcee34"],["q2-18acb584e232","s2.3-c2e2f8015078","2.3","Forms of Energy Important in Human Functioning","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe body uses the ultraviolet energy of sunlight to convert a ____ in skin cells to vitamin D, which is essential to human functioning.","compound",["product","protein","reactant"],"The body uses the ultraviolet energy of sunlight to convert a compound in skin cells to vitamin D, which is essential to human functioning.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1637609","f-1ab8d19ef0ac"],["q2-f8eef43ab6f7","s2.3-92aefdb02ffd","2.3","Characteristics of Chemical Reactions","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAll chemical reactions begin with a ____, the general term for the one or more substances that enter into the reaction.","reactant",["matter","concentration","molecule"],"All chemical reactions begin with a reactant, the general term for the one or more substances that enter into the reaction.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2081438","f-3cde4505f7bf"],["q2-e8aeac6593f3","s2.3-38a007151abe","2.3","Characteristics of Chemical Reactions","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe one or more substances produced by a chemical reaction are called the ____.","product",["protein","catalyst","concentration"],"The one or more substances produced by a chemical reaction are called the product.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2081438","f-0706d3947105"],["q2-db375091ba48","s2.3-38a007151abe","2.3","Characteristics of Chemical Reactions","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn chemical reactions, the components of the reactants—the elements involved and the number of atoms of each—are all present in the ____(s).","product",["protein","reactant","atom"],"In chemical reactions, the components of the reactants—the elements involved and the number of atoms of each—are all present in the product(s).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1383464","f-4645df536767"],["q2-0a3957bac8a7","s2.3-c6f57180e462","2.3","Characteristics of Chemical Reactions","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe chemical reaction in which one ____ of nitrogen and three atoms of hydrogen produce ammonia would be written as N + 3H → NH 3 N + 3H → NH 3.","atom",["reactant","protein","substrate"],"For example, the chemical reaction in which one atom of nitrogen and three atoms of hydrogen produce ammonia would be written as N + 3H → NH 3 N + 3H → NH 3.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2177278","f-696fbed67e3f"],["q2-0b29b6d776b5","s2.3-c2e2f8015078","2.3","Characteristics of Chemical Reactions","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nNotice that, in the first example, a nitrogen (N) atom and three hydrogen (H) atoms bond to form a ____.","compound",["catalyst","protein","product"],"Notice that, in the first example, a nitrogen (N) atom and three hydrogen (H) atoms bond to form a compound.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2069935","f-47dc688db52b"],["q2-41333fffb34d","s2.3-0cc5d96a99e2","2.3","Characteristics of Chemical Reactions","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAgain, nitrogen and hydrogen are reactants in a ____ that yields ammonia as the product.","synthesis reaction",["decomposition reaction","chemical energy","potential energy"],"Again, nitrogen and hydrogen are reactants in a synthesis reaction that yields ammonia as the product.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2069935","f-fddd329eaef7"],["q2-50d60ebe7f8a","s2.3-0cc5d96a99e2","2.3","Characteristics of Chemical Reactions","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe general equation for a ____ is A + B → AB.","synthesis reaction",["exchange reaction","kinetic energy","activation energy"],"The general equation for a synthesis reaction is A + B → AB.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2069935","f-c51fbba9d28d"],["q2-082d354c1584","s2.3-1fb42c942bd1","2.3","Characteristics of Chemical Reactions","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe general equation for a ____ is: AB → A + B AB → A + B.","decomposition reaction",["synthesis reaction","exchange reaction","potential energy"],"The general equation for a decomposition reaction is: AB → A + B AB → A + B.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2152461","f-2eba2c6ad0ec"],["q2-699960f8ed4b","s2.3-95855e4784d5","2.3","Characteristics of Chemical Reactions","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn ____ is a chemical reaction in which both synthesis and decomposition occur, chemical bonds are both formed and broken, and chemical energy is absorbed, stored, and released (see c ).","exchange reaction",["valence shell","activation energy","synthesis reaction"],"An exchange reaction is a chemical reaction in which both synthesis and decomposition occur, chemical bonds are both formed and broken, and chemical energy is absorbed, stored, and released (see c ).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2110634","f-7402d99c89e3"],["q2-7dcfac1c6d35","s2.3-95855e4784d5","2.3","Characteristics of Chemical Reactions","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe simplest form of an ____ might be: A + BC → AB + C A + BC → AB + C.","exchange reaction",["synthesis reaction","decomposition reaction","valence shell"],"The simplest form of an exchange reaction might be: A + BC → AB + C A + BC → AB + C.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2110634","f-34d2b5cb99c7"],["q2-ab75861bf713","s2.3-95855e4784d5","2.3","Characteristics of Chemical Reactions","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA more complex ____ might be: AB + CD → AC + BD AB + CD → AC + BD.","exchange reaction",["activation energy","valence shell","potential energy"],"A more complex exchange reaction might be: AB + CD → AC + BD AB + CD → AC + BD.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2110634","f-f4395df5a7eb"],["q2-48cc04b60a5c","s2.3-38a007151abe","2.3","Characteristics of Chemical Reactions","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nReactants may synthesize into a ____ that is later decomposed.","product",["matter","protein","reactant"],"Reactants may synthesize into a product that is later decomposed.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1618560","f-511a95a1d2e0"],["q2-232f7a144f9c","s2.3-b5d0c80a5b51","2.3","Properties of the Reactants","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nSimilarly, the larger the ____, the greater the number of total bonds, so reactions involving smaller molecules, with fewer total bonds, would be expected to proceed faster.","molecule",["bond","concentration","radioactive isotope"],"Similarly, the larger the molecule, the greater the number of total bonds, so reactions involving smaller molecules, with fewer total bonds, would be expected to proceed faster.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1289953","f-bc5ee4045598"],["q2-a0a795b740e8","s2.3-3ea71d906c7f","2.3","Temperature","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nRecall that ____ is the energy of matter in motion.","kinetic energy",["potential energy","activation energy","decomposition reaction"],"Recall that kinetic energy is the energy of matter in motion.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1752078","f-61fcf700d86a"],["q2-7347b5afc087","s2.3-66d80f5fad63","2.3","Concentration and Pressure","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIf there were 100 dancers in that club, and the manager abruptly moved the party to a room half the size, the ____ of the dancers would double in the new space, and the likelihood of collisions would increase accordingly.","concentration",["compound","acid","product"],"If there were 100 dancers in that club, and the manager abruptly moved the party to a room half the size, the concentration of the dancers would double in the new space, and the likelihood of collisions would increase accordingly.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2254825","f-d0a2abcf8ce2"],["q2-253e04393e00","s2.3-c6482363313d","2.3","Enzymes and Other Catalysts","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn chemistry, a ____ is a substance that increases the rate of a chemical reaction without itself undergoing any change.","catalyst",["atom","protein","acid"],"In chemistry, a catalyst is a substance that increases the rate of a chemical reaction without itself undergoing any change.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2371267","f-0c78581a3d17"],["q2-5629cfe8433d","s2.3-c6482363313d","2.3","Enzymes and Other Catalysts","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nYou can think of a ____ as a chemical change agent.","catalyst",["reactant","matter","molecule"],"You can think of a catalyst as a chemical change agent.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2371267","f-a3b8b7ead2e8"],["q2-67c670ab227c","s2.3-8b2e04d7ef91","2.3","Enzymes and Other Catalysts","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA chemical reaction’s ____ is the “threshold” level of energy needed to break the bonds in the reactants.","activation energy",["exchange reaction","kinetic energy","synthesis reaction"],"A chemical reaction’s activation energy is the “threshold” level of energy needed to break the bonds in the reactants.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1855242","f-29d06e458be5"],["q2-bd4395130ad3","s2.3-c6482363313d","2.3","Enzymes and Other Catalysts","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nWithout an enzyme to act as a ____, a much larger investment of energy is needed to ignite a chemical reaction.","catalyst",["matter","bond","product"],"Without an enzyme to act as a catalyst, a much larger investment of energy is needed to ignite a chemical reaction.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1855242","f-8a7e491bc178"],["q2-07056c184306","s2.4-e53c2ea1fe4b","2.4","Buffers","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsolution containing a weak acid or a weak base that opposes wide fluctuations in the pH of body fluids","buffer",["proton","electron","bond"],"buffer: solution containing a weak acid or a weak base that opposes wide fluctuations in the pH of body fluids.","The answer begins with B and has 1 word.","fs-id1282302","f-c9328a765d4a"],["q2-630dee77e813","s2.4-02a48f7219ec","2.4","Concentrations of Solutes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nliquid mixture in which the solute particles consist of clumps of molecules large enough to scatter light","colloid",["concentration","atom","reactant"],"colloid: liquid mixture in which the solute particles consist of clumps of molecules large enough to scatter light.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id1960900","f-22d51ffaecb9"],["q2-b23b697c5820","s2.4-3f0f73e2a32f","2.4","Inorganic Compounds Essential to Human Functioning","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsubstance that does not contain both carbon and hydrogen","inorganic compound",["covalent bond","protein","colloid"],"inorganic compound: substance that does not contain both carbon and hydrogen.","The answer begins with I and has 2 words.","fs-id2095610","f-5b8595ff96cd"],["q2-affc2682bd45","s2.4-d616e07e85aa","2.4","Inorganic Compounds Essential to Human Functioning","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsubstance that contains both carbon and hydrogen","organic compound",["covalent bond","product","buffer"],"organic compound: substance that contains both carbon and hydrogen.","The answer begins with O and has 2 words.","fs-id2095610","f-4377874ad035"],["q2-fd2bf85100aa","s2.4-8df74dda184c","2.4","The Concept of pH","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nnegative logarithm of the hydrogen ion (H⁺ ) concentration of a solution","pH",["acid","product","buffer"],"pH: negative logarithm of the hydrogen ion (H⁺ ) concentration of a solution.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id1358455","f-1b65ef35d0e0"],["q2-ce32e0aea375","s2.4-00f97cb506e7","2.4","Water as a Component of Liquid Mixtures","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nhomogeneous liquid mixture in which a solute is dissolved into molecules within a solvent","solution",["proton","buffer","atom"],"solution: homogeneous liquid mixture in which a solute is dissolved into molecules within a solvent.","The answer begins with S and has 1 word.","fs-id1856926","f-c5e676f94305"],["q2-996b8cc97ccb","s2.4-066b4eb7b442","2.4","Concentrations of Solutes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nliquid mixture in which particles distributed in the liquid settle out over time","suspension",["base","colloid","electron"],"suspension: liquid mixture in which particles distributed in the liquid settle out over time.","The answer begins with S and has 1 word.","fs-id2328232","f-97bfe03cb470"],["q2-551421d5da2c","s2.4-3f0f73e2a32f","2.4","Inorganic Compounds Essential to Human Functioning","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn ____ is a substance that does not contain both carbon and hydrogen.","inorganic compound",["covalent bond","matter","protein"],"An inorganic compound is a substance that does not contain both carbon and hydrogen.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2095610","f-8cd8a711a7f5"],["q2-6b7133ad582d","s2.4-d616e07e85aa","2.4","Inorganic Compounds Essential to Human Functioning","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn ____, then, is a substance that contains both carbon and hydrogen.","organic compound",["covalent bond","proton","element"],"An organic compound, then, is a substance that contains both carbon and hydrogen.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2095610","f-89fb382c9a1e"],["q2-c611e7fd386b","s2.4-c2e2f8015078","2.4","Water as a Component of Liquid Mixtures","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe room air you breathe is a gaseous mixture, containing three discrete elements—nitrogen, oxygen, and argon—and one ____, carbon dioxide.","compound",["suspension","colloid","bond"],"The room air you breathe is a gaseous mixture, containing three discrete elements—nitrogen, oxygen, and argon—and one compound, carbon dioxide.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2157424","f-acde354a9562"],["q2-32b7be55f5e1","s2.4-00f97cb506e7","2.4","Water as a Component of Liquid Mixtures","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn chemistry, a liquid ____ consists of a solvent that dissolves a substance called a solute.","solution",["reactant","bond","base"],"In chemistry, a liquid solution consists of a solvent that dissolves a substance called a solute.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1856926","f-d0bbf889342d"],["q2-da8322732445","s2.4-66d80f5fad63","2.4","Concentrations of Solutes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ of a given solute is the number of particles of that solute in a given space (oxygen makes up about 21 percent of atmospheric air).","concentration",["matter","proton","reactant"],"The concentration of a given solute is the number of particles of that solute in a given space (oxygen makes up about 21 percent of atmospheric air).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1218107","f-c1212fd5820c"],["q2-925f7a0a458a","s2.4-66d80f5fad63","2.4","Concentrations of Solutes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn the bloodstream of humans, glucose ____ is usually measured in milligram (mg) per deciliter (dL), and in a healthy adult averages about 100 mg/dL.","concentration",["protein","reactant","matter"],"In the bloodstream of humans, glucose concentration is usually measured in milligram (mg) per deciliter (dL), and in a healthy adult averages about 100 mg/dL.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1218107","f-ec24d5ebca37"],["q2-55ee7270a997","s2.4-00f97cb506e7","2.4","Concentrations of Solutes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA colloid is a mixture that is somewhat like a heavy ____.","solution",["suspension","concentration","pH"],"A colloid is a mixture that is somewhat like a heavy solution.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1960900","f-db009eb6fa90"],["q2-11ec543ee253","s2.4-02a48f7219ec","2.4","Concentrations of Solutes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn the thyroid glands, the thyroid hormone is stored as a thick protein mixture also called a ____.","colloid",["atom","pH","compound"],"In the thyroid glands, the thyroid hormone is stored as a thick protein mixture also called a colloid.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1960900","f-9ce378bd9f9d"],["q2-a1af0c2a491b","s2.4-066b4eb7b442","2.4","Concentrations of Solutes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is a liquid mixture in which a heavier substance is suspended temporarily in a liquid, but over time, settles out.","suspension",["compound","colloid","concentration"],"A suspension is a liquid mixture in which a heavier substance is suspended temporarily in a liquid, but over time, settles out.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2328232","f-718ddfc790ec"],["q2-ef0736bf3e8a","s2.4-00f97cb506e7","2.4","Salts","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAgain, whereas nonpolar and polar covalently bonded compounds break apart into molecules in ____, salts dissociate into ions.","solution",["atom","matter","reactant"],"Again, whereas nonpolar and polar covalently bonded compounds break apart into molecules in solution, salts dissociate into ions.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2103101","f-ae37fae94c3f"],["q2-57aaadc5d48d","s2.4-da005969e9d1","2.4","Acids","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nBecause an atom of hydrogen has just one proton and one ____, a positively charged hydrogen ion is simply a proton.","electron",["product","molecule","solution"],"Because an atom of hydrogen has just one proton and one electron, a positively charged hydrogen ion is simply a proton.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1938324","f-c8df4504409e"],["q2-d4e227faee30","s2.4-00f97cb506e7","2.4","Bases","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe hydroxyl ions (also known as hydroxide ions) or other basic substances combine with H⁺ present to form a water molecule, thereby removing H⁺ and reducing the ____’s acidity.","solution",["compound","matter","pH"],"The hydroxyl ions (also known as hydroxide ions) or other basic substances combine with H⁺ present to form a water molecule, thereby removing H⁺ and reducing the solution’s acidity.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1250576","f-17d8fb7abc0e"],["q2-517e3c4d311c","s2.4-00f97cb506e7","2.4","Bases","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nBicarbonate accepts some of the H⁺ protons, thereby reducing the acidity of the ____.","solution",["base","element","molecule"],"Bicarbonate accepts some of the H⁺ protons, thereby reducing the acidity of the solution.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1250576","f-c731649e2a75"],["q2-c2d59767f538","s2.4-66d80f5fad63","2.4","The Concept of pH","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA solution’s pH is the negative, base-10 logarithm of the hydrogen ion (H⁺ ) ____ of the solution.","concentration",["product","proton","suspension"],"A solution’s pH is the negative, base-10 logarithm of the hydrogen ion (H⁺ ) concentration of the solution.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1358455","f-ae94a3345637"],["q2-e664e1b78dac","s2.4-66d80f5fad63","2.4","The Concept of pH","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAs an example, a pH 4 solution has an H⁺ ____ that is ten times greater than that of a pH 5 solution.","concentration",["molecule","acid","compound"],"As an example, a pH 4 solution has an H⁺ concentration that is ten times greater than that of a pH 5 solution.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1358455","f-b903d4548b42"],["q2-a83185f8a375","s2.4-00f97cb506e7","2.4","The Concept of pH","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ with a pH of 7 is considered neutral—neither acidic nor basic.","solution",["buffer","product","atom"],"A solution with a pH of 7 is considered neutral—neither acidic nor basic.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1358455","f-41d2406edcd0"],["q2-e8d26eb21f26","s2.4-434b0a6daa53","2.4","Buffers","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAll cells of the body depend on homeostatic regulation of ____–base balance at a pH of approximately 7.4.","acid",["element","colloid","buffer"],"All cells of the body depend on homeostatic regulation of acid–base balance at a pH of approximately 7.4.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1282302","f-ee134853fcd6"],["q2-a3f52807fbfa","s2.4-e53c2ea1fe4b","2.4","Buffers","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ can neutralize small amounts of acids or bases in body fluids.","buffer",["solution","matter","proton"],"A buffer can neutralize small amounts of acids or bases in body fluids.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1282302","f-1bd0921122dc"],["q2-55d9ff9a22cd","s2.4-66d80f5fad63","2.4","Acids and Bases","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nLung disease, aspirin overdose, shock, and ordinary anxiety can cause respiratory alkalosis, which reduces the normal ____ of H⁺.","concentration",["proton","element","reactant"],"Lung disease, aspirin overdose, shock, and ordinary anxiety can cause respiratory alkalosis, which reduces the normal concentration of H⁺.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1897513","f-12c81a0f0563"],["q2-c520647dcb30","s2.5-060b6d0de851","2.5","Functions of Carbohydrates","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nnucleotide containing ribose and an adenine base that is essential in energy transfer","adenosine triphosphate (ATP)",["deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA)","ribonucleic acid (RNA)","denaturation"],"adenosine triphosphate (ATP): nucleotide containing ribose and an adenine base that is essential in energy transfer.","The answer begins with A and has 3 words.","fs-id1406008","f-9658e2d79bee"],["q2-557a65862918","s2.5-3ce49f9fe9c9","2.5","Microstructure of Proteins","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nbuilding block of proteins; characterized by an amino and carboxyl functional groups and a variable side-chain","amino acid",["peptide bond","valence shell","potential energy"],"amino acid: building block of proteins; characterized by an amino and carboxyl functional groups and a variable side-chain.","The answer begins with A and has 2 words.","fs-id2151470","f-ddc34b344246"],["q2-d4e2d29f4467","s2.5-d1402d57f45b","2.5","Carbohydrates","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nclass of organic compounds built from sugars, molecules containing carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen in a 1-2-1 ratio","carbohydrate",["bond","enzyme","steroid"],"carbohydrate: class of organic compounds built from sugars, molecules containing carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen in a 1-2-1 ratio.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id2160830","f-b43a29b0cdde"],["q2-b76c5fede5f4","s2.5-b74bce6fd065","2.5","Shape of Proteins","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nchange in the structure of a molecule through physical or chemical means","denaturation",["prostaglandin","substrate","base"],"denaturation: change in the structure of a molecule through physical or chemical means.","The answer begins with D and has 1 word.","fs-id1840952","f-37071f8cd080"],["q2-b92d85edb0c1","s2.5-71b1f358f937","2.5","Nucleic Acids","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndeoxyribose-containing nucleotide that stores genetic information","deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA)",["adenosine triphosphate (ATP)","ribonucleic acid (RNA)","triglyceride"],"deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA): deoxyribose-containing nucleotide that stores genetic information.","The answer begins with D and has 3 words.","fs-id2102405","f-136501f21349"],["q2-816fcc990eb9","s2.5-4a2758364bca","2.5","Disaccharides","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\npair of carbohydrate monomers bonded by dehydration synthesis via a glycosidic bond","disaccharide",["prostaglandin","atom","substrate"],"disaccharide: pair of carbohydrate monomers bonded by dehydration synthesis via a glycosidic bond.","The answer begins with D and has 1 word.","fs-id1363592","f-ff26e7b430fc"],["q2-7e6cbd3fa993","s2.5-43a20f5c0acc","2.5","Shape of Proteins","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncovalent bond formed within a polypeptide between sulfide groups of sulfur-containing amino acids, for example, cysteine","disulfide bond",["potential energy","functional group","amino acid"],"disulfide bond: covalent bond formed within a polypeptide between sulfide groups of sulfur-containing amino acids, for example, cysteine.","The answer begins with D and has 2 words.","fs-id1707170","f-e69521de00a3"],["q2-855d819945b3","s2.5-baf0261f2d74","2.5","The Chemistry of Carbon","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ngroup of atoms linked by strong covalent bonds that tends to behave as a distinct unit in chemical reactions with other atoms","functional group",["organic compound","covalent bond","valence shell"],"functional group: group of atoms linked by strong covalent bonds that tends to behave as a distinct unit in chemical reactions with other atoms.","The answer begins with F and has 2 words.","fs-id2069935","f-e2c3fcf83057"],["q2-5660ba07713c","s2.5-baca44e6b4ba","2.5","Lipids","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nclass of nonpolar organic compounds built from hydrocarbons and distinguished by the fact that they are not soluble in water","lipid",["prostaglandin","atom","molecule"],"lipid: class of nonpolar organic compounds built from hydrocarbons and distinguished by the fact that they are not soluble in water.","The answer begins with L and has 1 word.","fs-id1639379","f-b453855ce9e0"],["q2-cb33219fa432","s2.5-cc1ee7770e0a","2.5","The Chemistry of Carbon","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nlarge molecule formed by covalent bonding","macromolecule",["phosphorylation","product","prostaglandin"],"macromolecule: large molecule formed by covalent bonding.","The answer begins with M and has 1 word.","fs-id1383422","f-4cb070d7b8fb"],["q2-3e34ccfb5e8b","s2.5-a77204b92440","2.5","Monosaccharides","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmonomer of carbohydrate; also known as a simple sugar","monosaccharide",["prostaglandin","product","base"],"monosaccharide: monomer of carbohydrate; also known as a simple sugar.","The answer begins with M and has 1 word.","fs-id1618375","f-cf4598d1bf78"],["q2-3a6bbf18679f","s2.5-5a6d10f7e846","2.5","Microstructure of Proteins","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncovalent bond formed by dehydration synthesis between two amino acids","peptide bond",["potential energy","organic compound","functional group"],"peptide bond: covalent bond formed by dehydration synthesis between two amino acids.","The answer begins with P and has 2 words.","fs-id1700797","f-f880a0bcea13"],["q2-876ebde954bc","s2.5-30ffe84a5039","2.5","Phospholipids","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\na lipid compound in which a phosphate group is combined with a diglyceride","phospholipid",["molecule","protein","product"],"phospholipid: a lipid compound in which a phosphate group is combined with a diglyceride.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id1956733","f-c81c9ba1a5d0"],["q2-31ff921dc5d0","s2.5-08eb245a00ce","2.5","Adenosine Triphosphate","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\naddition of one or more phosphate groups to an organic compound","phosphorylation",["atom","phospholipid","carbohydrate"],"phosphorylation: addition of one or more phosphate groups to an organic compound.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id1845224","f-9052c019be20"],["q2-001d6dead414","s2.5-c7f1df1fa652","2.5","Polysaccharides","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncompound consisting of more than two carbohydrate monomers bonded by dehydration synthesis via glycosidic bonds","polysaccharide",["steroid","macromolecule","molecule"],"polysaccharide: compound consisting of more than two carbohydrate monomers bonded by dehydration synthesis via glycosidic bonds.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id1401240","f-5b598306c21d"],["q2-8e91fdf7af53","s2.5-4c8af4a9b66d","2.5","Phospholipids","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nlipid compound derived from fatty acid chains and important in regulating several body processes","prostaglandin",["triglyceride","reactant","substrate"],"prostaglandin: lipid compound derived from fatty acid chains and important in regulating several body processes.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fig-ch02_05_06","f-b1e5ab38cec0"],["q2-0cd1d8f9f509","s2.5-74e1f9819f56","2.5","Nucleic Acids","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nnitrogen-containing base with a double ring structure; adenine and guanine","purine",["macromolecule","steroid","bond"],"purine: nitrogen-containing base with a double ring structure; adenine and guanine.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id2045437","f-a5dab68fc5a7"],["q2-fc1876642108","s2.5-8d09a1f6df87","2.5","Steroids","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\n(also, sterol) lipid compound composed of four hydrocarbon rings bonded to a variety of other atoms and molecules","steroid",["solution","nucleotide","purine"],"steroid: (also, sterol) lipid compound composed of four hydrocarbon rings bonded to a variety of other atoms and molecules.","The answer begins with S and has 1 word.","fs-id1472124","f-9493418421e3"],["q2-974e679ad6ac","s2.5-bd0deb83a763","2.5","Proteins Function as Enzymes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nreactant in an enzymatic reaction","substrate",["triglyceride","purine","product"],"substrate: reactant in an enzymatic reaction.","The answer begins with S and has 1 word.","fs-id1861297","f-52a07eeb9f4f"],["q2-fa7e061005d3","s2.5-5374413726cf","2.5","Triglycerides","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nlipid compound composed of a glycerol molecule bonded with three fatty acid chains","triglyceride",["substrate","polysaccharide","concentration"],"triglyceride: lipid compound composed of a glycerol molecule bonded with three fatty acid chains.","The answer begins with T and has 1 word.","fs-id1386190","f-acbb29354e9e"],["q2-906e804d0202","s2.5-d616e07e85aa","2.5","The Chemistry of Carbon","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nMany combinations are possible to fill carbon’s four “vacancies.” Carbon may share electrons with oxygen or nitrogen or other atoms in a particular region of an ____.","organic compound",["covalent bond","functional group","amino acid"],"Many combinations are possible to fill carbon’s four “vacancies.” Carbon may share electrons with oxygen or nitrogen or other atoms in a particular region of an organic compound.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2069935","f-863f278ee7be"],["q2-f9ee59f4892c","s2.5-baf0261f2d74","2.5","The Chemistry of Carbon","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is a group of atoms linked by strong covalent bonds and tending to function in chemical reactions as a single unit.","functional group",["covalent bond","valence shell","peptide bond"],"A functional group is a group of atoms linked by strong covalent bonds and tending to function in chemical reactions as a single unit.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2069935","f-068107725620"],["q2-3cd5cc7be83d","s2.5-d1402d57f45b","2.5","Carbohydrates","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe term ____ means “hydrated carbon.” Recall that the root hydro- indicates water.","carbohydrate",["reactant","enzyme","concentration"],"The term carbohydrate means “hydrated carbon.” Recall that the root hydro- indicates water.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2160830","f-4b059435ec50"],["q2-d176300909ef","s2.5-d1402d57f45b","2.5","Carbohydrates","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn fact, the chemical formula for a “generic” molecule of ____ is (CH 2 O) n.","carbohydrate",["purine","steroid","denaturation"],"In fact, the chemical formula for a “generic” molecule of carbohydrate is (CH 2 O) n.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2160830","f-2dec4064e67a"],["q2-9afd23829ca9","s2.5-9671dede9244","2.5","Carbohydrates","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ are the polymers, and can consist of hundreds to thousands of monomers.","Polysaccharides",["enzyme","base","disaccharide"],"Polysaccharides are the polymers, and can consist of hundreds to thousands of monomers.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2371267","f-60288b1565c5"],["q2-3cd630e7e9cb","s2.5-9671dede9244","2.5","Polysaccharides","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ can contain a few to a thousand or more monosaccharides.","Polysaccharides",["denaturation","compound","solution"],"Polysaccharides can contain a few to a thousand or more monosaccharides.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2327023","f-eb6428106975"],["q2-83e0cab71a64","s2.5-c7f1df1fa652","2.5","Polysaccharides","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nCellulose, a ____ that is the primary component of the cell wall of green plants, is the component of plant food referred to as “fiber”.","polysaccharide",["lipid","protein","substrate"],"Cellulose, a polysaccharide that is the primary component of the cell wall of green plants, is the component of plant food referred to as “fiber”.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1401240","f-04a80741ccc9"],["q2-f5d04b6e2d17","s2.5-d1402d57f45b","2.5","Functions of Carbohydrates","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nGrains, fruits, and legumes and other vegetables provide most of the ____ in the human diet, although lactose is found in dairy products.","carbohydrate",["macromolecule","lipid","compound"],"Grains, fruits, and legumes and other vegetables provide most of the carbohydrate in the human diet, although lactose is found in dairy products.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1915226","f-3fc1dbc18338"],["q2-eb241bbbcfe0","s2.5-060b6d0de851","2.5","Functions of Carbohydrates","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is composed of a ribose sugar, an adenine base, and three phosphate groups.","adenosine triphosphate (ATP)",["deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA)","ribonucleic acid (RNA)","covalent bond"],"Adenosine triphosphate (ATP) is composed of a ribose sugar, an adenine base, and three phosphate groups.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1406008","f-425cd3af828e"],["q2-5c729b615596","s2.5-d1402d57f45b","2.5","Functions of Carbohydrates","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nSome ____ molecules bind with proteins to produce glycoproteins, and others combine with lipids to produce glycolipids, both of which are found in the membrane that encloses the contents of body cells.","carbohydrate",["protein","steroid","compound"],"For instance, some carbohydrate molecules bind with proteins to produce glycoproteins, and others combine with lipids to produce glycolipids, both of which are found in the membrane that encloses the contents of body cells.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1617913","f-d75523a45adb"],["q2-020166f3d633","s2.5-baca44e6b4ba","2.5","Lipids","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is one of a highly diverse group of compounds made up mostly of hydrocarbons.","lipid",["reactant","phosphorylation","macromolecule"],"A lipid is one of a highly diverse group of compounds made up mostly of hydrocarbons.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1639379","f-99f8620097c7"],["q2-a82708fdc198","s2.5-5374413726cf","2.5","Triglycerides","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is one of the most common dietary lipid groups, and the type found most abundantly in body tissues.","triglyceride",["molecule","base","protein"],"A triglyceride is one of the most common dietary lipid groups, and the type found most abundantly in body tissues.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1386190","f-9676e2b7ca08"],["q2-b14f5ac781bd","s2.5-e45a817e7453","2.5","Triglycerides","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nWhen you are resting or asleep, a majority of the energy used to keep you alive is derived from ____ stored in your fat (adipose) tissues.","triglycerides",["acid","macromolecule","carbohydrate"],"When you are resting or asleep, a majority of the energy used to keep you alive is derived from triglycerides stored in your fat (adipose) tissues.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2339167","f-645188bbd03a"],["q2-8e50630d3edf","s2.5-2c65275ae0b0","2.5","Triglycerides","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nLipoproteins are compounds in which the hydrophobic triglycerides are packaged in ____ envelopes for transport in body fluids.","protein",["prostaglandin","macromolecule","nucleotide"],"Lipoproteins are compounds in which the hydrophobic triglycerides are packaged in protein envelopes for transport in body fluids.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2181771","f-389a8e46a8f4"],["q2-8d619f564bea","s2.5-30ffe84a5039","2.5","Phospholipids","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe head of a ____ contains charges on the phosphate groups, as well as on the nitrogen atom.","phospholipid",["reactant","compound","phosphorylation"],"However, the head of a phospholipid contains charges on the phosphate groups, as well as on the nitrogen atom.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1956733","f-d7bb923a527a"],["q2-e0e978ba7ca7","s2.5-c6f57180e462","2.5","Phospholipids","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nPhospholipids are said to have hydrophobic tails, containing the neutral fatty acids, and hydrophilic heads, containing the charged phosphate groups and nitrogen ____.","atom",["nucleotide","base","radioactive isotope"],"Therefore, phospholipids are said to have hydrophobic tails, containing the neutral fatty acids, and hydrophilic heads, containing the charged phosphate groups and nitrogen atom.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1956733","f-206986fed7bd"],["q2-22d4113ad84a","s2.5-4c8af4a9b66d","2.5","Prostaglandins","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nLike a hormone, a ____ is one of a group of signaling molecules, but prostaglandins are derived from unsaturated fatty acids (see c ).","prostaglandin",["reactant","concentration","disaccharide"],"Like a hormone, a prostaglandin is one of a group of signaling molecules, but prostaglandins are derived from unsaturated fatty acids (see c ).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2263987","f-ec7290e85e4e"],["q2-f6e245c73602","s2.5-e51c498b65bf","2.5","Proteins","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nYou might associate proteins with ____, but in fact, proteins are critical components of all tissues and organs.","muscle tissue",["potential energy","amino acid","organic compound"],"You might associate proteins with muscle tissue, but in fact, proteins are critical components of all tissues and organs.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2271940","f-20252cade488"],["q2-adff48a47b4d","s2.5-b5d0c80a5b51","2.5","Proteins","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA protein is an organic ____ composed of amino acids linked by peptide bonds.","molecule",["carbohydrate","triglyceride","bond"],"A protein is an organic molecule composed of amino acids linked by peptide bonds.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2271940","f-d0e63efe54ad"],["q2-9d27705440bd","s2.5-bafd3e0b93aa","2.5","Proteins","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nProteins include the keratin in the ____ of skin that protects underlying tissues, the collagen found in the dermis of skin, in bones, and in the meninges that cover the brain and spinal cord.","epidermis",["protein","concentration","acid"],"Proteins include the keratin in the epidermis of skin that protects underlying tissues, the collagen found in the dermis of skin, in bones, and in the meninges that cover the brain and spinal cord.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2271940","f-6d76351d2550"],["q2-a0afad42c4ed","s2.5-3ce49f9fe9c9","2.5","Microstructure of Proteins","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn ____ is a molecule composed of an amino group and a carboxyl group, together with a variable side chain.","amino acid",["functional group","valence shell","peptide bond"],"An amino acid is a molecule composed of an amino group and a carboxyl group, together with a variable side chain.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2151470","f-61e69e72cdc4"],["q2-78565e05ae79","s2.5-434b0a6daa53","2.5","Microstructure of Proteins","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nNotice that all amino acids contain both an ____ (the carboxyl group) and a base (the amino group) (amine = “nitrogen-containing”).","acid",["polysaccharide","concentration","phospholipid"],"Notice that all amino acids contain both an acid (the carboxyl group) and a base (the amino group) (amine = “nitrogen-containing”).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1698290","f-61bee436e1e9"],["q2-6803e0647fda","s2.5-2c65275ae0b0","2.5","Microstructure of Proteins","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAmino acids join via dehydration synthesis to form ____ polymers.","protein",["reactant","polysaccharide","denaturation"],"Amino acids join via dehydration synthesis to form protein polymers.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1700797","f-6b02675c6c3c"],["q2-656c9c0e1359","s2.5-5a6d10f7e846","2.5","Microstructure of Proteins","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe unique bond holding amino acids together is called a ____.","peptide bond",["amino acid","disulfide bond","valence shell"],"The unique bond holding amino acids together is called a peptide bond.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1700797","f-2284fdc23cef"],["q2-63a982d48c70","s2.5-1bdf81c422c2","2.5","Microstructure of Proteins","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA peptide bond is a ____ between two amino acids that forms by dehydration synthesis.","covalent bond",["organic compound","valence shell","disulfide bond"],"A peptide bond is a covalent bond between two amino acids that forms by dehydration synthesis.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1700797","f-8fa25ebf64df"],["q2-14c065e2efa2","s2.5-3ce49f9fe9c9","2.5","Microstructure of Proteins","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nFree amino acids available for protein construction are said to reside in the ____ pool within cells.","amino acid",["organic compound","disulfide bond","valence shell"],"Free amino acids available for protein construction are said to reside in the amino acid pool within cells.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2143872","f-e476ae4cb89d"],["q2-52385376e353","s2.5-2c65275ae0b0","2.5","Shape of Proteins","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe secondary structure of proteins further folds into a compact three-dimensional shape, referred to as the ____’s tertiary structure (see c ).","protein",["molecule","macromolecule","triglyceride"],"The secondary structure of proteins further folds into a compact three-dimensional shape, referred to as the protein’s tertiary structure (see c ).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1707170","f-b31c5fcd8e45"],["q2-d68c3fc7f46e","s2.5-43a20f5c0acc","2.5","Shape of Proteins","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is a covalent bond between sulfur atoms in a polypeptide.","disulfide bond",["functional group","valence shell","peptide bond"],"A disulfide bond is a covalent bond between sulfur atoms in a polypeptide.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1707170","f-5e9406dcc3a0"],["q2-ccb2b1e52296","s2.5-2c65275ae0b0","2.5","Shape of Proteins","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nOften, two or more separate polypeptides bond to form an even larger ____ with a quaternary structure (see d ).","protein",["substrate","product","compound"],"Often, two or more separate polypeptides bond to form an even larger protein with a quaternary structure (see d ).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1707170","f-37c746038959"],["q2-5e931bdd2a25","s2.5-b045bba3839c","2.5","Shape of Proteins","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ subunits forming a quaternary structure can be identical or different.","polypeptide",["prostaglandin","protein","denaturation"],"The polypeptide subunits forming a quaternary structure can be identical or different.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1707170","f-2c6e14a89ece"],["q2-9cb2e7f7b2a6","s2.5-2c65275ae0b0","2.5","Shape of Proteins","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nHemoglobin, the ____ found in red blood cells is composed of four tertiary polypeptides, two of which are called alpha chains and two of which are called beta chains.","protein",["prostaglandin","denaturation","acid"],"For instance, hemoglobin, the protein found in red blood cells is composed of four tertiary polypeptides, two of which are called alpha chains and two of which are called beta chains.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1707170","f-2d46de6c43d9"],["q2-6933ab2aa381","s2.5-b74bce6fd065","2.5","Shape of Proteins","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is a change in the structure of a molecule through physical or chemical means.","Denaturation",["lipid","atom","substrate"],"Denaturation is a change in the structure of a molecule through physical or chemical means.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1840952","f-d6b5b05939f4"],["q2-6c21ab67ba41","s2.5-b74bce6fd065","2.5","Shape of Proteins","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn everyday example of protein ____ is the curdling of milk when acidic lemon juice is added.","denaturation",["reactant","atom","monosaccharide"],"An everyday example of protein denaturation is the curdling of milk when acidic lemon juice is added.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1840952","f-3d054c86a6f6"],["q2-991982402d80","s2.5-1e24e38c6148","2.5","Proteins Function as Enzymes","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nEnzymatic reactions—chemical reactions catalyzed by enzymes—begin when substrates bind to the ____.","enzyme",["solution","disaccharide","denaturation"],"Enzymatic reactions—chemical reactions catalyzed by enzymes—begin when substrates bind to the enzyme.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1861297","f-0632d8e8ce69"],["q2-11ec298761d0","s2.5-bd0deb83a763","2.5","Proteins Function as Enzymes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is a reactant in an enzymatic reaction.","substrate",["concentration","solution","triglyceride"],"A substrate is a reactant in an enzymatic reaction.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1861297","f-dbb500e374cc"],["q2-3524d3a58fc2","s2.5-1e24e38c6148","2.5","Proteins Function as Enzymes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAny given ____ catalyzes just one type of chemical reaction.","enzyme",["polysaccharide","macromolecule","atom"],"Any given enzyme catalyzes just one type of chemical reaction.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1861297","f-154cbba6a6f6"],["q2-e111db412949","s2.5-bd0deb83a763","2.5","Proteins Function as Enzymes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe enzyme is then free to engage in the process again, and will do so as long as ____ remains.","substrate",["monosaccharide","macromolecule","atom"],"The enzyme is then free to engage in the process again, and will do so as long as substrate remains.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1645297","f-d124c4b1086d"],["q2-0362b00753ca","s2.5-d1402d57f45b","2.5","Other Functions of Proteins","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe body can use proteins for energy when ____ and fat intake is inadequate, and stores of glycogen and adipose tissue become depleted.","carbohydrate",["molecule","concentration","prostaglandin"],"The body can use proteins for energy when carbohydrate and fat intake is inadequate, and stores of glycogen and adipose tissue become depleted.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2070161","f-89268e340747"],["q2-e1ac742d0fe4","s2.5-d616e07e85aa","2.5","Nucleotides","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe fourth type of ____ important to human structure and function are the nucleotides.","organic compound",["potential energy","disulfide bond","valence shell"],"The fourth type of organic compound important to human structure and function are the nucleotides.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1433784","f-4d519ec0d83e"],["q2-ef134a7f2d83","s2.5-31f1e25a1b1d","2.5","Nucleotides","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is one of a class of organic compounds composed of three subunits:","nucleotide",["polysaccharide","prostaglandin","base"],"A nucleotide is one of a class of organic compounds composed of three subunits.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1433784","f-649cf3cf4b4d"],["q2-6350e214c215","s2.5-1405df66cbe2","2.5","Nucleotides","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA nitrogen-containing ____: adenine, cytosine, guanine, thymine, or uracil","base",["molecule","nucleotide","macromolecule"],"a nitrogen-containing base: adenine, cytosine, guanine, thymine, or uracil.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2254187","f-d1042310e642"],["q2-87bbe0c492dc","s2.5-c2e2f8015078","2.5","Nucleotides","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nNucleotides can be assembled into nucleic acids (DNA or RNA) or the energy ____ adenosine triphosphate.","compound",["solution","substrate","lipid"],"Nucleotides can be assembled into nucleic acids (DNA or RNA) or the energy compound adenosine triphosphate.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1371433","f-cb87769370fd"],["q2-1a04097c031e","s2.5-1405df66cbe2","2.5","Nucleic Acids","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe “choices” of ____ for DNA are adenine, cytosine, guanine, and thymine.","base",["polysaccharide","compound","substrate"],"The “choices” of base for DNA are adenine, cytosine, guanine, and thymine.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2102405","f-cad3d6bdbf00"],["q2-0588979d2477","s2.5-cdbcbd2f1208","2.5","Nucleic Acids","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is a ribose-containing nucleotide that helps manifest the genetic code as protein.","ribonucleic acid (RNA)",["adenosine triphosphate (ATP)","deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA)","macromolecule"],"Ribonucleic acid (RNA) is a ribose-containing nucleotide that helps manifest the genetic code as protein.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2102405","f-e3bb0da8776c"],["q2-49e6465bb47f","s2.5-b5d0c80a5b51","2.5","Nucleic Acids","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA purine is a nitrogen-containing ____ with a double ring structure, which accommodates several nitrogen atoms.","molecule",["substrate","bond","prostaglandin"],"A purine is a nitrogen-containing molecule with a double ring structure, which accommodates several nitrogen atoms.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2045437","f-a87c4d0148de"],["q2-396f4e3bc4da","s2.5-3f0f7e68f822","2.5","Nucleic Acids","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is a nitrogen-containing base with a single ring structure","pyramidine",["phospholipid","steroid","concentration"],"A pyramidine is a nitrogen-containing base with a single ring structure.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2045437","f-928932deceb4"],["q2-d9544576c4aa","s2.5-060b6d0de851","2.5","Adenosine Triphosphate","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe nucleotide ____, is composed of a ribose sugar, an adenine base, and three phosphate groups.","adenosine triphosphate (ATP)",["deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA)","ribonucleic acid (RNA)","lipid"],"The nucleotide adenosine triphosphate (ATP), is composed of a ribose sugar, an adenine base, and three phosphate groups.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2626876","f-d089a7b12213"],["q2-a6f92a3106fc","s2.5-4995b8cb5536","2.5","Organic Compounds Essential to Human Functioning","connections","According to the textbook table on Organic Compounds Essential to Human Functioning, for functional group: Hydroxyl, which option gives the structural formula?","—O—H",["—NH 2","—CH 3"],"functional group: Hydroxyl. Structural formula: —O—H.","Match the named structure or substance to the property asked about; do not use another row or column.","tbl-ch02_01","f-f6f06dcc4c0e"],["q2-62176fd0b7b4","s2.5-77d77f5f4e0e","2.5","Organic Compounds Essential to Human Functioning","connections","According to the textbook table on Organic Compounds Essential to Human Functioning, for functional group: Hydroxyl, which option gives the importance?","Hydroxyl groups are polar. They are components of all four types of organic compounds discussed in this chapter. They are involved in dehydration synthesis and hydrolysis reactions.",["Amino groups are found within amino acids, the building blocks of proteins.","Methyl groups are found within amino acids.","Carboxyl groups are found within fatty acids, amino acids, and many other acids."],"functional group: Hydroxyl. Importance: Hydroxyl groups are polar. They are components of all four types of organic compounds discussed in this chapter. They are involved in dehydration synthesis and hydrolysis reactions..","Match the named structure or substance to the property asked about; do not use another row or column.","tbl-ch02_01","f-9d9e03626423"],["q2-f3e9f21688c3","s2.5-de2bb7a4ca61","2.5","Organic Compounds Essential to Human Functioning","connections","According to the textbook table on Organic Compounds Essential to Human Functioning, for functional group: Carboxyl, which option gives the importance?","Carboxyl groups are found within fatty acids, amino acids, and many other acids.",["Hydroxyl groups are polar. They are components of all four types of organic compounds discussed in this chapter. They are involved in dehydration synthesis and hydrolysis reactions.","Methyl groups are found within amino acids.","Amino groups are found within amino acids, the building blocks of proteins."],"functional group: Carboxyl. Importance: Carboxyl groups are found within fatty acids, amino acids, and many other acids..","Match the named structure or substance to the property asked about; do not use another row or column.","tbl-ch02_01","f-bb7ae744d025"],["q2-47638084fd71","s2.5-94d4cc69961c","2.5","Organic Compounds Essential to Human Functioning","connections","According to the textbook table on Organic Compounds Essential to Human Functioning, for functional group: Amino, which option gives the structural formula?","—NH 2",["—O—H","—CH 3"],"functional group: Amino. Structural formula: —NH 2.","Match the named structure or substance to the property asked about; do not use another row or column.","tbl-ch02_01","f-1394ebbd86b3"],["q2-72a434b2ec99","s2.5-a7766deba725","2.5","Organic Compounds Essential to Human Functioning","connections","According to the textbook table on Organic Compounds Essential to Human Functioning, for functional group: Amino, which option gives the importance?","Amino groups are found within amino acids, the building blocks of proteins.",["Carboxyl groups are found within fatty acids, amino acids, and many other acids.","Methyl groups are found within amino acids.","Hydroxyl groups are polar. They are components of all four types of organic compounds discussed in this chapter. They are involved in dehydration synthesis and hydrolysis reactions."],"functional group: Amino. Importance: Amino groups are found within amino acids, the building blocks of proteins..","Match the named structure or substance to the property asked about; do not use another row or column.","tbl-ch02_01","f-1cacd9e4a582"],["q2-4448fd1b233f","s2.5-64604fa3820b","2.5","Organic Compounds Essential to Human Functioning","connections","According to the textbook table on Organic Compounds Essential to Human Functioning, for functional group: Methyl, which option gives the structural formula?","—CH 3",["—O—H","—NH 2"],"functional group: Methyl. Structural formula: —CH 3.","Match the named structure or substance to the property asked about; do not use another row or column.","tbl-ch02_01","f-cf9e634996e7"],["q2-d186b22828de","s2.5-9d2794e7ed7e","2.5","Organic Compounds Essential to Human Functioning","connections","According to the textbook table on Organic Compounds Essential to Human Functioning, for functional group: Methyl, which option gives the importance?","Methyl groups are found within amino acids.",["Carboxyl groups are found within fatty acids, amino acids, and many other acids.","Amino groups are found within amino acids, the building blocks of proteins.","Hydroxyl groups are polar. They are components of all four types of organic compounds discussed in this chapter. They are involved in dehydration synthesis and hydrolysis reactions."],"functional group: Methyl. Importance: Methyl groups are found within amino acids..","Match the named structure or substance to the property asked about; do not use another row or column.","tbl-ch02_01","f-86493744908e"],["q2-f1dd6aa043f9","s2.3-f692f2f9f911","2.3","Factors Influencing the Rate of Chemical Reactions","connections","Which change generally increases reaction rate by making reactant collisions more frequent?","Increasing reactant concentration",["Removing every reactant","Lowering all molecular motion","Eliminating collisions"],"More reactant particles in a given space increases the opportunity for collisions.","Identify the structure or mechanism named in the question, then compare the alternatives.","fs-id2654903","f-760b078b5969"],["q2-2f41c2eeb4ea","s2.3-2566f3526f90","2.3","Factors Influencing the Rate of Chemical Reactions","application","Two reactions use equal amounts of a solid reactant. Why may powdered material react faster than a single large piece?","Powder exposes more surface area for interactions",["Powder contains no atoms","A large piece has no chemical bonds","Surface area cannot affect reaction rate"],"Breaking a solid into smaller pieces increases exposed surface area without changing its chemical identity.","Identify the structure or mechanism named in the question, then compare the alternatives.","fs-id2654903","f-8b1cabff2d8d"],["q2-edit-ed513e16e18c","s2.1-edit-ea861b11ee43","2.1","Interventional Radiologist","connections","Which activity best describes interventional radiology?","Using imaging to guide minimally invasive treatment",["Studying only the chemical composition of dietary fats","Replacing every imaging examination with open surgery","Producing red blood cells in bone marrow"],"Interventional radiologists use imaging-guided, minimally invasive procedures to diagnose or treat selected conditions.","Use the role or mechanism described in the question.","fs-id1909147","f-812bf236465c"],["q2-edit-98d65349a4a4","s2.4-edit-7fbc0f509a65","2.4","Water","connections","Where is much of the body's water located?","Both inside cells and in extracellular fluid",["Only inside the stomach","Only in blood cells and nowhere else","Only within bone crystals"],"Body water is distributed between intracellular and extracellular compartments.","Use the role or mechanism described in the question.","fs-id1893156","f-b81dc0d16630"],["q2-edit-393eb1d02c1d","s2.4-edit-f8944aeeb9b8","2.4","Water as a Lubricant and Cushion","connections","How does water in synovial fluid help a joint?","It helps lubricate movement between joint surfaces",["It turns cartilage into skeletal muscle","It prevents all movement at the joint","It supplies the electrical insulation of nerves"],"Water contributes to lubricating fluids that reduce friction during movement.","Use the role or mechanism described in the question.","fs-id2364434","f-693384e57497"],["q2-edit-324953983725","s2.4-edit-1b00801310bd","2.4","Water as a Heat Sink","connections","Why can body water help limit rapid temperature changes?","It absorbs substantial heat with a relatively small temperature change",["It cannot absorb thermal energy","It generates heat only by freezing","It prevents sweat from evaporating"],"Water has a high heat capacity, and evaporation of water can also remove heat from the body.","Use the role or mechanism described in the question.","fs-id1616535","f-178531b74240"],["q2-edit-e88caff4b231","s2.4-edit-bf29fcc7384f","2.4","The Role of Water in Chemical Reactions","connections","In a hydrolysis reaction, what role does water play?","Water is consumed when a chemical bond is broken",["Water is produced as two monomers form a new bond","Water serves as the enzyme that catalyzes every reaction","Water is converted directly into a carbon atom"],"Hydrolysis uses water to split a bond; dehydration synthesis releases water when forming a bond.","Use the role or mechanism described in the question.","fs-id1368540","f-d6f237195bce"]]);
